Based on the event history data, during the post-mortem discussion, the team identified that **mandatory real-time use case review** could have caught this issue before it affected customers.

Specifically, Alice stated (line 323-326):
> "Post-mortem follow-up: I think we need to add a mandatory 'real-time use case review' step to any architecture changes involving caching or data delays. We should have caught this during the design phase."

The team realized that they optimized for performance by implementing a 2-hour cache TTL without properly analyzing how different customer segments used the dashboard. E-commerce clients running flash sales needed real-time data (sub-minute freshness), but the blanket 2-hour caching made their data essentially useless for time-sensitive decisions like inventory management during Black Friday sales.

The prevention method identified was to make **real-time use case analysis a mandatory step** before implementing any architecture changes that could affect data freshness or introduce delays.
